# Generic login-required copy

When a page, empty fill, modal, callout, or alert exists only because the user must log in to use a
feature, the body is **one generic sentence**. Do not sell the feature or explain what they would
get after signing in.

Canonical catalog key: **`authentication.login_required`**
(“Log in to use this feature.” and the same wording in other locales).

## Do

- Use `authentication.login_required` for new login-gated fills, web `ModalLoginRequired` bodies,
  and similar CTAs. Existing `instructions.login_to_*` / `features.*.login_prompt` keys must carry
  that same generic sentence — do not write a unique blurb per feature.
- Keep the **Login** action (`authentication.login`). Hide a duplicate in-body heading; the
  screen/modal title is enough.
- Keep titles like “Login Required” (`instructions.login_required`) as titles, not as the body.

## Don't

- Do not write feature-specific “why log in” copy (synced inbox, playlists you can share, never miss
  an episode, “log in to send boosts”, and the like).
- Do not use a different login-required sentence on web than on mobile.

## Not this rule

- Session-expired / re-auth errors (`authentication.session_expired`).
- Membership / Premium gates (`membership.gate.*`, Trial limits). Those are not login-required
  empties.
- Helper notes that explain a feature **after** the user is signed in.

## Related

- **mobile-screen-layout** — login-gated fill uses `CallToActionSection` / `AuthAwareLoadState`
- **i18n-user-facing-strings** — resolve the string through the catalog
